")
<chapter id="arrayref">
#title("Linear Arrays")

#para("\
\
#emphasis("Note:") the first versions of this chapter described persistent
arrays (the type #stacode("arrayref")), which are reclaimed by garbage
collection (GC). That type was removed when the support for GC was dropped:
the compiler now rejects every nonlinear heap allocation. The arrays that
are allocated on heap are linear now: the type for them is
#stacode("arrayptr") and they must be freed explicitly. The interface is in
#myatscodelink("prelude/SATS/arrayptr.sats", "prelude/SATS/arrayptr.sats").
\
")

#para("\

A linear array of size n is n consecutive cells in the heap together with
the obligation to free them. Given a viewtype VT, the type for linear
arrays containing N values of viewtype VT is #stacode("arrayptr(VT, N)").
Note that arrays in ATS are the same as those in C: There is no size
information attached to them at run-time, and the size of an array is only
part of its static type. (The chapter on arrays-with-size shows how to
keep the size and the array together.) Since the type is linear, the
compiler makes sure that an array is either freed or passed to a function
that takes over the obligation; it also rejects any attempt to use an array
that has been freed already.

")#comment("para")

#para("\

There are various functions in ATSLIB for array creation. For instance, the
following two are commonly used:

#sats2xhtml('\
fun
{a:t0p}
arrayptr_make_elt
  {n:int}
  (asz: size_t(n), x: a):<!wrt> arrayptr(a, n)
// end of [arrayptr_make_elt]

fun
{a:vt0p}
arrayptr_make_list_vt{n:int}
  ( asz: int(n)
  , xs0: list_vt(INV(a), n)):<!wrt> arrayptr(a, n)
// end of [arrayptr_make_list_vt]
')

Applied to a size and an element, #dyncode("arrayptr_make_elt") returns an
array of the given size in which each cell is initialized with the given
element.  Applied to a (linear) list of elements and the length of the list,
#dyncode("arrayptr_make_list_vt") consumes the list and returns an array of
size equal to the given length in which each cell is initialized with the
corresponding element in the given list.

")

#para("\

The memory of an array of values of a non-linear type is released by
calling the function #dyncode("arrayptr_free") (the symbol
#dyncode("free") is overloaded with it):

#sats2xhtml('\
fun
arrayptr_free
  {a:t0p}
  {l:addr}{n:int}
  (A: arrayptr(INV(a), l, n)):<!wrt> void = "mac#%"
// end of [arrayptr_free]
')

If the array contains linear values, they have to be taken care of first,
for instance, by calling #dyncode("arrayptr_freelin"), which clears the
cells one by one with the user-supplied template
#dyncode("array_uninitize$clear") before it frees the array.

")

#para("\

For reading from and writing to an array, the function templates
#dyncode("arrayptr_get_at") and #dyncode("arrayptr_set_at") can be used,
respectively, which are assigned the following interfaces (we show the
ones for the indices of unsigned integer types such as #stacode("size_t")):

#sats2xhtml('\
fun{
a:t0p}{tk:tk
} arrayptr_get_at_guint
  {n:int}{i:nat | i < n}
  (A: !arrayptr(INV(a), n), i: g1uint (tk, i)):<> (a)
//
fun{
a:t0p}{tk:tk
} arrayptr_set_at_guint
  {n:int}{i:nat | i < n}
  (A: !arrayptr(INV(a), n), i: g1uint (tk, i), x: a):<!wrt> void
')

Note that the symbol #keycode("!") in front of the type of the array
indicates that the array is only borrowed: it is still available after a
call returns. Given an array #dyncode("A"), an index #dyncode("i") and a
value #dyncode("v"), #dyncode("arrayptr_get_at(A, i)") and
#dyncode("arrayptr_set_at(A, i, v)") can be written as #dyncode("A[i]") and
#dyncode("A[i] := v"), respectively. The index is checked statically
against the size of the array: there is no run-time test, and an access
that cannot be proven to be in bounds is a type-error.\

")

#para("

As an example, the following function template reverses the content of a
given array:\

#dats2xhtml('\
fun{a:t0p}
arrayptr_reverse{n:nat}
(
  A: !arrayptr(a, n), n: size_t(n)
) : void = let
//
fun loop
  {i: nat | i <= n} .<n-i>.
(
  A: !arrayptr(a, n), n: size_t n, i: size_t i
) : void = let
  val n2 = half (n)
in
  if i < n2 then let
    val tmp = A[i]
    val ni = pred(n)-i
    val x = A[ni]
    val () = A[i] := x
    val () = A[ni] := tmp
  in
    loop(A, n, succ(i))
  end else () // end of [if]
end // end of [loop]
//
in
  loop(A, n, i2sz(0))
end // end of [arrayptr_reverse]
')

")

#para("\

If the test #dyncode("i < n2") is changed to #dyncode("i <= n2"), a type-error
is to be reported. Why? The reason is that #dyncode("A[n-1-i]") becomes
out-of-bounds array subscripting in the case where #dyncode("n") and
#dyncode("i") both equal zero. Given that it is very unlikely to encounter a
case where an array of size 0 is involved, a bug like this, if not detected
early, can be buried so scarily deep!\

")

#para("\

The careful reader may have already noticed that the sort #dyncode("t0p")
is assigned to the template parameter #dyncode("a"). In other words, the
above implementation of #dyncode("arrayptr_reverse") cannot handle a case
where the values stored in a given array are of some linear type. The
reason for choosing the sort #dyncode("t0p") is that both
#dyncode("arrayptr_get_at") and #dyncode("arrayptr_set_at") can only be
applied to an array containing values of a nonlinear type: reading a linear
value out of a cell would duplicate it. In the following
implementation, the template parameter is given the sort #dyncode("vt0p")
so that an array containing linear values, that is, values of some linear
type can be handled:\

")

#dats2xhtml('\
fun{a:vt0p}
arrayptr_reverse_lin{n:nat}
(
  A: !arrayptr(a, n), n: size_t(n)
) : void = let
//
fun loop
  {i: nat | i <= n} .<n-i>.
(
  A: !arrayptr(a, n), n: size_t n, i: size_t i
) : void = let
  val n2 = half (n)
in
  if i < n2 then let
    val () = arrayptr_interchange(A, i, pred(n)-i) in loop(A, n, succ(i))
  end else () // end of [if]
end // end of [loop]
//
in
  loop(A, n, i2sz(0))
end // end of [arrayptr_reverse_lin]
')

#para('

The interface for the function template #dyncode("arrayptr_interchange") is
given below:

#sats2xhtml('\
fun{a:vt0p}
arrayptr_interchange
  {n:int}
(
  A: !arrayptr(INV(a), n), i: sizeLt n, j: sizeLt n
) :<!wrt> void // end of [arrayptr_interchange]
')

Note that #dyncode("arrayptr_interchange") can not be implemented in terms
of #dyncode("arrayptr_get_at") and #dyncode("arrayptr_set_at") (unless some
form of type-unsafe code is employed).\

')

#para('\

There are various functions available for traversing an array from left to
right or from right to left, such as #dyncode("arrayptr_foreach") and
#dyncode("arrayptr_rforeach"). Programming with an explicit index is also
fine. For instance, the fold-left function for arrays can be implemented
as follows:

#dats2xhtml('\
fun{a,b:t0p}
arrayptr_foldleft{n:nat}
(
  f: (a, b) -> a, x: a, A: !arrayptr(b, n), n: size_t(n)
) : a = let
//
fun loop
  {i: nat | i <= n} .<n-i>.
(
  f: (a, b) -> a
, x: a, A: !arrayptr(b, n), n: size_t(n), i: size_t(i)
) : a =
(
if i < n
  then loop(f, f(x, A[i]), A, n, succ(i)) else x
) (* end of [loop] *)
//
in
  loop(f, x, A, n, i2sz(0))
end // end of [arrayptr_foldleft]
')

')

#para('\

The following test creates an array, fills it, reverses it twice, sums up
its elements, and frees it:

#dats2xhtml('\
val n = i2sz(10)
val A = arrayptr_make_elt<int>(n, 0)
//
fun
fill{n:nat}{i:nat | i <= n} .<n-i>.
  (A: !arrayptr(int, n), n: size_t(n), i: size_t(i)): void =
  if i < n then (A[i] := sz2i(i); fill(A, n, succ(i)))
//
val () = fill(A, n, i2sz(0))
val () = arrayptr_reverse(A, n)
val () = (fprint_arrayptr<int>(stdout_ref, A, n); print_newline())
val () = arrayptr_reverse_lin(A, n)
val () = (fprint_arrayptr<int>(stdout_ref, A, n); print_newline())
val sum = arrayptr_foldleft<int,int>(lam (x, y) => x + y, 0, A, n)
val () = println! ("sum(A) = ", sum)
val () = arrayptr_free(A)
')

')

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("chap_arrayptr.dats", "on-line") the entirety of the
code used in this chapter.\

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--"arrayref"-->

